WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.180
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:02.980
So I'm on vacation in Michigan right now,

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:05.680
and the days here unspool in a kind of slow motion.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.280
I read, and write, and hang out with my family

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.980
and stare out at this Great Lake, which, to the human eye, might as well be an ocean,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.400
since it stretches up all the way to the horizon. I love a good horizon line.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.880
I remember as a kid wondering what would happen if you sailed out to the horizon.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.780
I mean, I knew the world was round and everything, but it sure looked like it ended.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.920
Like if you sailed far enough, you'd be in the sky.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.660
And then, as I got older, I realized you could chase the horizon forever and never actually get to it,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.060
which gave me this weird kind of comfort I find it hard to describe.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.640
One of my favorite artists is the Japanese photographer Hiroshi Sugimoto,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.740
and he has a series of photographs called "Seascapes," where he photographed water horizons all over the world,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:48.560
from the Tasman Sea, to the Carribean, to the Sea of Japan, to the Baltic, to the Mediterranean, to Lake Superior.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.060
Sugimoto has said that water and air "vouchsafe for our very existence."

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.920
And maybe that's the comfort I feel looking out at a water horizon. Maybe I feel my existence "vouchsafed."

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.520
These days, seeing the hardline of the horizon makes me think not about the edge of the world, but about the edgelessness of it.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.680
"The world is a globe," Terry Pratchett wrote, "the farther you sail, the closer to home you are."

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.600
Like, our dad grew up in Indianapolis, back when its nickname was "Nap Town" because nothing ever happened there.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.820
It was a place that attracted people who actively wanted to live quiet lives in the flat and unbeautiful middle of America.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:27.020
The kind of place Nick Carraway could retreat to at the end of "The Great Gatsby" after being unable to bear the awful excitement of New York.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:33.700
Our dad worked like hell to get out of Indianapolis. He and Mom left for good a few weeks after I was born, and never looked back.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.040
And then in 2007, Sarah and I were living in New York, and I called Dad with the news.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:45.600
Sarah had gotten a job, I told him, in...hmm...well...in Indianapolis. And he said, "You can't be serious."

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:53.400
I'd sailed away from a birthplace I never really knew, to Orlando and Birmingham, Chicago and New York, and ended up right back where I started.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.760
I used to think of that as a failure, that I'd ended up back in the middle.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.980
Midwesterners sometimes call the Great Lakes "the Third Coast," or we brag that our great sea is unsalted and sharkless.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.540
But we know that our lakes are not as grand as the coastal oceans.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:13.400
I felt I had failed because I wasn't looking out at the greatest horizon, or swimming with the strongest current, or battling the biggest waves.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.800
I used to think that even though you could never get to the horizon, you had to keep chasing it your whole life,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.400
that, in fact, the point of life was to chase after the unreachable horizon.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.080
I used to think that "Moby Dick"'s Captain Ahab, who loses everything in pursuit of that great white whale,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.600
was the only proper hero in American fiction.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.440
But now, Hank, I find myself content, utterly content, to look at the horizon rather than chase after it.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.440
I like the middle. I'm happy here, at this lake which can trick my eyes into believing it goes on forever.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.440
I think I've underrated happiness, and also underrated the pleasure of just observing.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.720
So thanks for watching with me, Hank. I'll see you on Friday.

